+BLOCKED: web.go request-ID chain order needs an owner fix
+
+# T-102 — CSRF plumbing + middleware guards (M1)
+
+The block is narrow and precise: **every packet deliverable is implemented,
+tested, committed and green**. What is blocked is one clause of A-105 — the
+"request id" field of the served log — because satisfying it requires
+re-arranging the middleware chain inside `internal/web/web.go`, which this
+task's `owns` forbid me to edit (packet: "web.go is NOT in your owns — do not
+edit it. If you believe it needs a change, write BLOCKED instead"). This is
+that write.
+
+## The defect, reproduced this session
+
+`web.go` `Handler()` composes the chain id-assigner-inner, logger-outer:
+
+- `web.go:62` `h = s.withRequestID(h)` (inner)
+- `web.go:63` `h = s.withLogging(h)` (outer)
+
+`withRequestID` enriches the context by rebinding its **local** request
+(`web.go:117` `r = r.WithContext(...)`), so the outer `withLogging` still holds
+the pre-enrichment request when it logs (`web.go:141`
+`requestIDOf(r.Context())`), and `context.go:28-33` returns the `"-"` no-id
+sentinel. The response header `X-Request-Id` carries the real id, so every
+served request line disagrees with its own header. Observed this session, in
+the keeper test (run `go test ./internal/web/ -run TestWithLoggingRequestFields -v`):
+
+```
+middleware_logging_test.go:128: served-chain req_id = "-"
+```
+
+The same is true of the real binary: `cmd/server/main.go:43` installs
+`srv.Handler()` unchanged, so prod logs every request with `req_id: "-"`.
+A-105's contract line (`acceptance/contract.json:34-37`) requires a request id
+in the log; `-` is the explicit no-id sentinel, not one.
+
+## Why the tree is green while the defect stands
+
+The scheduler hard-fails on `make check-fast` before reviews run
+(`cmd/harness/sched.go:250-253`), and the merge gate runs `make check`
+(`gate.go:103`), so a deliberately failing unit test would prevent this task
+from ever merging — taking the CSRF API that T-103 depends on down with it.
+The review verdict itself sanctions the alternative: "BLOCKED … with a failing
+test **or an explicit non-coverage note**". This handoff is that note, and the
+non-coverage is also documented in code:
+
+- `TestWithLoggingRequestFields` / `jsonRequestLine` assert the strongest
+  condition that can honestly pass through the served chain today
+  (`req_id` present), with an on-purpose comment explaining the gap and a
+  `-v` log line that prints the observed sentinel value, so the defect stays
+  visible and any run of the suite documents it.
+- **`TestWithLoggingLogsRealRequestID` (new this attempt) is the real guard
+  the review demanded**: it composes `withRequestID` outside `withLogging`
+  over a real mux and asserts the logged `req_id` **equals the response
+  `X-Request-Id`** and is a real 16-hex id (regex `^[0-9a-f]{16}$`), i.e.
+  never `-`. It passes, proving both middleware units already satisfy the
+  A-105 request-id contract; only the production arrangement in `web.go`
+  does not. If either unit regresses (logger stops logging the context id,
+  assigner stops setting header or context), this test goes red.
+
+## The owner fix (one line, in `web.go` `Handler()` only)
+
+Swap the two compositions so the id exists before the logger runs:
+
+```go
+h = s.withLogging(h)      // was line 63
+h = s.withRequestID(h)    // was line 62
+```
+
+(An equivalent alternative is having `withLogging` read
+`rec.Header().Get("X-Request-Id")` after `next.ServeHTTP`.) After the swap:
+`TestWithLoggingLogsRealRequestID` keeps guarding the units, the `-v` line in
+`TestWithLoggingRequestFields` starts printing a real id, and the served chain
+satisfies A-105's request-id clause end to end. Nothing in T-102's files needs
+to change. Optionally (owner's call — the acceptance suite is protected, not
+mine): strengthen `acceptance/go/m1_guards_test.go:275-277` from
+key-presence to the real-id shape; today it passes on `-` too.
+
